WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.083
Find the capabilities. Name business responsibilities before drawing services. Catalog descriptions and authoritative seat reservations have different ownership and correctness requirements.

2
00:00:37.083 --> 00:01:16.250
Own the data. Each capability owns its protected state and exposes contracts. Giving every service write access to every table defeats much of the intended independence.

3
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:58.833
Cross a network boundary. The booking workflow contacts payments. That call can time out or succeed remotely while its response is lost; it is not a local transaction.

4
00:01:58.833 --> 00:02:36.250
Decouple optional work. Confirmation can publish notification work after reliable business-state coordination. A slow email provider need not occupy the booking request indefinitely.

5
00:02:36.250 --> 00:03:10.583
Deploy compatibly. Old and new callers may coexist. Test contract compatibility and observe version-specific errors instead of requiring every service to deploy in lockstep.

6
00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:52.667
Justify the complexity. For a small team, a modular monolith may satisfy the same domain boundaries with fewer moving parts. Choose services from requirements rather than fashion.
